{
 "F1": {
  "cause": "Not the code (the simulator and runner files have one commit each, older than both run sets) and not the library (both sets record the same torch build). The installed torch's CUDA random kernels size their grid by the GPU's multiprocessor count and threads per multiprocessor (header lines below), and the Philox subsequence and offset of every drawn element follow the grid; the generators of memsim_rev.Streams live on the GPU, so the same seed gives a different initialisation and a different torch.randperm example order on a GPU of another size. The A4000 and the P2000 differ in both. The differences between the runs are therefore different random realisations, not rounding of one realisation. Consistent with this, the probe runs' CIFAR and LM example orders are equal between the A4000 and the P2200 while their initialisations and the MNIST order are not (probe_runs_vs_e3): a draw is device-independent only where the drawing kernel's grid does not depend on the device. Prediction checked by experiment A2-M: the P2200 (other multiprocessor count) gives hashes equal across its own three arms and different from both the A4000's and the P2000's.",
  "claim": "seed 0 82.02 % (A4000) vs 89.69 % (P2000) is attributed to 'GPU rounding alone'; the five-seed A2-R join mixes matched and unmatched pairs",
